The notebook includes an acc(coeffs) implementation that injects randomness during evaluation: [/evidence/artifacts/acc_and_predicts.txt:28-32]. It shows:
  def acc(coeffs):
      noisy = calc_preds(coeffs, trn_indep) + torch.rand_like(trn_dep.float())*0.01
      return (trn_dep.bool()==(noisy>0.5)).float().mean()

The code also shows that train_model seeds PyTorch before training (torch.manual_seed(442)) [/evidence/artifacts/acc_and_predicts.txt:21-23], which only affects training determinism; it does not remove the random draw inside acc(). Because acc() explicitly uses torch.rand_like during evaluation, accuracy values will vary between runs unless the RNG is reseeded before each acc() call. Therefore the repository does not satisfy the test that accuracy is deterministic when evaluating multiple times without retraining.

The notebook defines a non-deterministic accuracy function [cp3501-prac06-task01-linear-model-by-hand.ipynb cell 98], which is saved here for easy verification: [/evidence/artifacts/acc-cell.txt]. The relevant code in that file:

def acc(coeffs):
    idx = torch.randperm(len(val_indep))
    rand_preds = torch.rand(len(val_indep))
    return (val_dep[idx].bool()==(rand_preds>0.5)).float().mean()

This implementation clearly uses torch.randperm and torch.rand at evaluation time to generate random indices and random predictions, so accuracy will vary between runs. For context, training sets a seed inside train_model (cell 85) via torch.manual_seed(442) [/evidence/artifacts/train-model-cell.txt], but that does not make repeated calls to acc deterministic because acc itself generates fresh random numbers on each call (and no reseeding is performed before evaluation).
